abstract: >
    Benchmark data for Indonesian and Javanese language models, covering slang
    comprehension, text normalisation, spelling correction, Javanese speech
    levels, and Javanese word meanings. Open-ended items are graded by a native
    speaker under a published rubric.

    Most sets withhold part of their items from publication so that figures stay
    defensible against training-set contamination. The held-back slice lives
    inside the set it belongs to and runs on the same pass as the published part,
    so the gap between the two measures leakage rather than drift. Its scores are
    reported; its keys are not. Sets that publish an answer key say so and carry a
    contamination canary.

    Counts move as sets grow, so read them from the data rather than from this
    abstract. Each dataset folder carries a PROVENANCE file stating what is
    published, what is withheld, and what the figures cannot show. See README.md
    for the limits of each set and CONTRIBUTING.md for how to dispute a figure.
